add('chn_2002_3968c7',4.5,'A gcd normalization supplies the missing divisibility of x by y, after which exponential growth restricts the quotient to two small cases.',r'''If $x\le y$, the equation forces $(x,y)=(1,1)$; if $y=1$, it gives the same pair. Otherwise $x>y\ge2$, and dividing by $y^y$ yields $(x/y)^y=y^{x-2y}>1$, so $x>2y$.

Write $x=da$, $y=db$ with $\gcd(a,b)=1$. Taking the $d$th root of the original equation and cancelling gives
$$a^b=d^{a-2b}b^{a-b}.$$
Since $a>2b$, any prime divisor of $b$ would divide $a$, impossible. Thus $b=1$, so $x=ky$ with $k\ge3$. The equation reduces to $k=y^{k-2}$. For $k\ge5$, $2^{k-2}>k$ by induction, so only $k=3,4$ remain. They give $y=3,2$, respectively. The complete answer is $\boxed{(x,y)=(1,1),(9,3),(8,2)}$.''',statement=r'''Determine all positive integer pairs $(x,y)$ such that $x^y=y^{x-y}$.''',topic='Number Theory',subtopics=['Diophantine equations','Divisibility & GCD','Squares & powers'],note='Supplied the gcd argument missing from the source claim that y divides x.')
add('chn_2002_1bc8f5',5,'Rotating a projection direction partitions the circle at pairwise coincidence directions; an explicit distinct-slope configuration attains the maximum and order regions cannot repeat.',r'''Only the direction of the line matters. A projection order can change only when the direction becomes perpendicular to a line joining two points. There are at most $\binom82=28$ such unoriented directions, hence at most $56$ open arcs of allowed oriented directions. The order is constant on each arc, giving an upper bound of $56$.

For attainment choose $A_i=(2^i,4^i)$ for $1\le i\le8$. The slope of $A_iA_j$ is $2^i+2^j$, and all 28 slopes are distinct. Thus there are 56 direction arcs. Two distinct arcs cannot give the same order: for any fixed strict order, its allowable direction vectors are the intersection of the open half-planes $(A_{i_{j+1}}-A_{i_j})\cdot v>0$. This is a convex cone contained in a half-plane, so its intersection with the direction circle is a single arc. Consequently all 56 orders are different. The maximum is $\boxed{56}$.''',statement=r'''Choose eight labeled points in the plane. For each oriented line on which their orthogonal projections are all distinct, read the labels in projection order. What is the greatest possible number of different permutations obtained as the oriented line varies?''',topic='Combinatorics',subtopics=['Combinatorial geometry','Permutations & arrangements','Extremal arguments'])
add('chn_2024_9e1916',7,'After bounding the orbit, the minimum large state identifies a universal small state; the return path alternates except for one consecutive-small transition, proving uniqueness and odd period.',r'''Let $F$ be the given update map. For $1/(\alpha-1)<u\le L$, $F(u)>u$; for $v>L$, the hypothesis on $L$ gives $F(v)>L/\alpha-1>1/(\alpha-1)$ and $F(v)<v$. Thus the orbit stays in a finite set of positive integers and is eventually periodic.

Consider any periodic cycle. Its largest value is at most $\lfloor\alpha L\rfloor$, and every value above $L$ is followed by one below $L$. Some value must exceed $L$, since updates below $L$ strictly increase. Let $v$ be the smallest cycle value above $L$ and put $u=\lfloor v/\alpha\rfloor$. We have $\lfloor\alpha u\rfloor<v$, so minimality of $v$ forces $w=\lfloor\alpha u\rfloor\le L$. Hence $u<(L+1)/\alpha$, while $v\ge L+1$ gives $u\ge\lfloor(L+1)/\alpha\rfloor$. Thus every cycle contains the same number
$$u=\left\lfloor\frac{L+1}{\alpha}\right\rfloor.$$
All cycles therefore coincide, as the update is deterministic, proving independence of the initial value.

This $u$ is the smallest value of the cycle: every small block starts after a large value at least $v$, and then increases. For any cycle value $t$ with $u<t\le L$, one has $\lfloor\alpha t\rfloor>L$, and its following value is $\lfloor\lfloor\alpha t\rfloor/\alpha\rfloor<t$. The cycle therefore starts $u,w$ with $u<w\le L$, then alternates large and small values until it returns to $u$. Exactly one step joins two small values; all remaining steps alternate, so the minimal cycle length is odd.''',statement=r'''Fix irrational $\alpha>1$ and an integer $L>\alpha^2/(\alpha-1)$. Start from an integer $x_1>L$ and define $x_{n+1}=\lfloor\alpha x_n\rfloor$ when $x_n\le L$, and $x_{n+1}=\lfloor x_n/\alpha\rfloor$ otherwise. Prove that the sequence is eventually periodic and that its least eventual period is odd and independent of $x_1$.''',topic='Number Theory',subtopics=['Sequences & recurrences','Floors & fractional parts','Extremal arguments'])
add('chn_2024_56a823',6.5,'The common point is a hidden midpoint; reflection of tangency points proves one incidence, while a diameter circle and a perpendicular-bisector reflection prove the other.',r'''Let $K$ be the midpoint of $ID$. The midpoint theorem in triangle $AIC$ shows $K\in LN$.

Reflect $D$ in $E$ and $F$ to obtain $X$ and $Y$. If $Z$ is the incircle contact point of $ABC$ on $AC$, tangent lengths give
$$CX=2DE+CD=AD+BD-AB+CD=AC+BC-AB=2CZ.$$
Thus $Z$ is the midpoint of $CX$, and $IX=IC$. Since $CI$ bisects $\angle ACB$, we obtain $\angle IXC=\tfrac12\angle ACB$. Also $DE=DF$ makes $DX=DY$, and $BD=BC$ gives $\angle YXC=\tfrac12\angle BDC=\tfrac12\angle ACB$. The points $I,X,Y$ are therefore collinear. In triangle $DXY$, the midpoint line $EF$ contains the midpoint $K$ of $DI$.

Since $JM\perp AC$, the circle $DMJ$ has diameter $DJ$, so $DQ\perp JQ$. The line $JL$ is the perpendicular bisector of $AI$, hence $DQ\parallel AI$. Reflect $D$ in $JL$ to obtain $T$; because $Q\in JL$, it is the midpoint of $DT$. The same reflection interchanges $A,I$, so it sends $AD$ to $IT$. The midpoint theorem gives $QK\parallel TI$, and consequently $\angle DQK=180^\circ-\angle IAD$ in the corresponding directed sense. On the other hand, cyclicity and $MP\parallel AI$ give $\angle PQD=\angle PMD=\angle IAD$. Therefore $P,Q,K$ are collinear. All three lines pass through $K$.''',statement=r'''Let $I$ be the incenter of triangle $ABC$, and let $L,M,N$ be the midpoints of $AI,AC,CI$. Suppose $D$ lies inside $AM$ and $BD=BC$. The incircle of $ABD$ touches $AD,BD$ at $E,F$. Let $J$ be the circumcenter of $AIC$. The circle through $D,M,J$ meets $MN$ again at $P$ and $JL$ again at $Q$. Prove that $EF,PQ,LN$ are concurrent.''',topic='Geometry',subtopics=['Triangle centres','Transformations','Concurrency & collinearity','Circles & tangency'])
